# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-liberty-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-4.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-4.logic.r1.3 have "outside_criticism → outside_ability" and "engross → ¬outside_ability" and "efficient → progressive"; mill-liberty-4.logic.r1.2 has "outside_criticism → outside_ability" and "engross → absorb" and "efficient → progressive"; they differ when only "the mental activity and progressiveness of the governing body, its ability kept up to a high standard" and "the governing body engrosses all the occupations which form and cultivate the faculties required for the government of mankind" are true, which the first allows
mill-liberty-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: mill-liberty-4.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-4.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if the governing body engrosses all the occupations which form and cultivate the faculties required for the government of mankind, then it is not so that the means exist, independently of the government, of forming such ability and furnishing it with the opportunities and experience necessary for a correct judgment of great practical affairs"; mill-liberty-4.logic.r1.2 does not
mill-liberty-4.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps mill-liberty-4.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-4.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the governing body engrosses all the occupations which form and cultivate the faculties required for the government of mankind, then it is not so that the means exist, independently of the government, of forming such ability and furnishing it with the opportunities and experience necessary for a correct judgment of great practical affairs"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
mill-liberty-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: mill-liberty-4.logic.r1.2 takes for granted "if the governing body engrosses all the occupations which form and cultivate the faculties required for the government of mankind, then the absorption of all the principal ability of the country into the governing body"; mill-liberty-4.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-4.logic.r1.3 do not
mill-liberty-4.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps mill-liberty-4.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the governing body engrosses all the occupations which form and cultivate the faculties required for the government of mankind, then the absorption of all the principal ability of the country into the governing body"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
